            <ArticleTitle>Trigeminal nerve palsy and Horner's syndrome following epidural analgesia for labor: a subdural block?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>180-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Horner's syndrome is a rare complication of epidural analgesia for labor. Much more uncommon is trigeminal nerve palsy. Both complications may be attributed to a subdural localization of the catheter, as we demonstrated clinically by a repeat injection and patchy sympathetic block and with the typical image on epidurography.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
